cff-version: 1.2.0
type: dataset
title: "Evidence Synthesis Automation Map"
message: "If you use this map or its benchmark, please cite it using the metadata in this file."
abstract: >-
  A maintained map of automation in evidence synthesis. For 26 steps of a systematic review or
  meta-analysis it records which tools already do the work, what they cost, and what they leave to
  the human, with a check date on every row. It also carries a measured probe of how much of the same
  work a web-enabled large language model already does: nine tasks set from real published material,
  with the method, the scoring rules, the results and the limits. The answer keys were built from the
  primary sources by a separate agent before the model under test saw anything, which is not the same
  as an independent second human extractor; on one task the model found something a key had missed
  and that key was corrected before scoring. This is a first public version, and rows will be added
  and corrected as tools change.
